Different services can access an S3 bucket with different methods. The post from John Humphreys tells about one condition. In this condition, code that a user controls accesses the bucket, and this code operates in the same AWS account. These are some other access patterns:

- Cloudfront supplies data from the bucket: there is an item, the "Origin Access Identity". It is a type of role, special for Cloudfront, that can access the S3 bucket. You cannot attach policies to this role. Thus, you must use the S3 bucket policy as a security layer that overlaps. This policy gives the role access to the bucket (also with all other access that there is).

  One more example is a Wordpress plugin that this person used. Before, this plugin had to have an S3 bucket and objects that all persons can open. In this condition, you must configure the policy to give access to the Origin Access Identity only.

- Think about a SaaS service that supplies data to your bucket. This service does not want limits on how it does that. At this time, the service possibly uses EC2. But it wants to be free to supply the data with Lambda, or with S3 bucket replication, at a different time. In this condition, you set a policy that lets all items in the other AWS account have write access to your bucket.

- Some AWS services have a default role with a name. For example, if you use EC2 Lifecycle Manager, the service makes a role in your account with the name AWSDataLifecycleManagerDefaultRole. If this role must use a KMS key, the key must have a policy that gives access to this role.

- This person has not used S3 inventory. But look at the example policy that the information page of the service has a link to: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies.html#example-bucket-policies-use-case-9

```
{  
        "Version": "2012-10-17",
        "Statement": [
          {
              "Sid": "InventoryAndAnalyticsExamplePolicy",
              "Effect": "Allow",
              "Principal": {
                  "Service": "s3.amazonaws.com"
              },
              "Action": "s3:PutObject",
              "Resource": [
                  "arn:aws:s3:::destinationbucket/*"
              ],
              "Condition": {
                  "ArnLike": {
                      "aws:SourceArn": "arn:aws:s3:::sourcebucket"
